## [GAP ANALYSIS] ECDSA test uses one curve for all three algorithms and never verifies a signature

**Severity:** LOW - the only test for the hand-written ASN.1 encoder/decoder would not catch a wrong conversion

### The Problem

```java
keyPair = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("EC").genKeyPair();
...
byte[] rsConcat = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().asn1derToConcatenatedRS(sign, algorithm.getSignatureLength());
byte[] asn1Des  = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().concatenatedRSToASN1DER(rsConcat, algorithm.getSignatureLength());
byte[] rsConcat2 = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().asn1derToConcatenatedRS(asn1Des, algorithm.getSignatureLength());
Assert.assertArrayEquals(rsConcat, rsConcat2);
```

1. One key pair on the JDK's default curve is used for ES256, ES384 and ES512. The default EC key size depends on the JDK (P-256 on JDK 17; later JDKs raised the default to P-384 under JDK-8267319). So for at least two of the three algorithms, `r`/`s` do not match `signLength / 2`.
2. The assertion compares the output of `asn1derToConcatenatedRS` with itself after a round trip. If `integerToBytes` truncates (`qLength < bytes.length`, line 153 of `AuthzClientCryptoProvider`), both sides are truncated the same way and the test still passes.
3. No signature is ever checked with `Signature.verify`, so an encoder that produces invalid DER that its own decoder accepts would also pass.

### Theory / Proof

For ES256 with a P-384 key, `r` and `s` are about 48 bytes but `len` is 32. `integerToBytes` keeps the low 32 bytes, which loses data. `rsConcat` and `rsConcat2` are then equal, and the test reports success. On JDK 17 with a P-256 key, ES384/ES512 only exercise the zero-padding branch, never the real 48/66-byte P-384/P-521 cases (P-521 is where `toByteArray()` lengths vary the most).

### What happens today vs. what will break

The conversion code looks correct today, but the test cannot prove it. A regression in `ASN1Encoder.writeLength` for long-form lengths (P-521 DER signatures are over 127 bytes, so they need the `0x81` long form) would go undetected, because on P-256 the sequence is always under 128 bytes.

### Research

The server-side `BCECDSACryptoProvider` gets its correctness from BouncyCastle's DER classes. This PR replaces them with custom code, so it needs stronger tests. Keycloak's own ECDSA tests in the testsuite generate keys per curve (`secp256r1`, `secp384r1`, `secp521r1`).

### Recommendation

Use the matching curve for each algorithm and verify the re-encoded DER with the JDK:

```java
private void test(ECDSAAlgorithm algorithm, String curve) throws Exception {
    KeyPairGenerator kpg = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("EC");
    kpg.initialize(new ECGenParameterSpec(curve));
    KeyPair keyPair = kpg.genKeyPair();
    AuthzClientCryptoProvider prov = new AuthzClientCryptoProvider();
    byte[] data = "Something to sign".getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
    Signature signature = Signature.getInstance(JavaAlgorithm.getJavaAlgorithm(algorithm.name()));
    signature.initSign(keyPair.getPrivate());
    signature.update(data);
    byte[] sign = signature.sign();
    byte[] rs = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().asn1derToConcatenatedRS(sign, algorithm.getSignatureLength());
    Assert.assertEquals(algorithm.getSignatureLength(), rs.length);
    byte[] der = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().concatenatedRSToASN1DER(rs, algorithm.getSignatureLength());
    Signature verifier = Signature.getInstance(JavaAlgorithm.getJavaAlgorithm(algorithm.name()));
    verifier.initVerify(keyPair.getPublic());
    verifier.update(data);
    Assert.assertTrue(verifier.verify(der));
}

@Test public void testES256() throws Exception { test(ECDSAAlgorithm.ES256, "secp256r1"); }
@Test public void testES384() throws Exception { test(ECDSAAlgorithm.ES384, "secp384r1"); }
@Test public void testES512() throws Exception { test(ECDSAAlgorithm.ES512, "secp521r1"); }
```
